{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/348279-praising-sts","id":348279,"title":"Praising STs","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-23T07:16:26-08:00","id":348279,"title":"Praising STs","content":"Ok, so we have the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/290174-bitching-about-st-thread\">Bitching about ST</a> thread, we have the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/303168-bitching-about-players-thread\">Bitching about Players</a> thread. But what about STs who have just been good, or great? Maybe not consistently, we can't all be that lucky, but we all know STs who have run really solid games, or even just great sessions. Or maybe they just dealt with a difficult situation very well. This thread is about those times.<br /> <br /> This ST, who I shall name John, was mostly a player in my first RPG group. Pretty quiet guy, but a really good friend. Anyway, he put his hand up to run a W:tF game set in medieval times. All right, says us. One of the players wanted to play a werebear instead of an Uratha, probably because he wanted to feel special (sidenote: this player was the same guy who ran the D&amp;D game I mentioned in the BAST thread. Guy was a tool). One player was a nobleman, my character was the daughter of a wealthy merchant who, for various reasons, pretty much retreated into the Shadow after her father died, and the other was a werebear with oddly modern - and <i>quite </i>liberal modern, at that - sensibilities.<br /> <br /> The ST was really good. He'd set it up so that we had three days to get to a particular meeting about two days hard travel away, and had planned for us to encounter two things on the way down, with each thing taking up a session, then once we get down there the main story began (he intended us to be late). We skipped the first, on the basis it didn't seem like a quick solve and it'd been impressed on us that this was important, and the second we kind of got dragged into (but in a good way, not a railroady way). All told, we ended up getting there most of a day late, which our characters were kind of upset about, as we didn't want to shame our pack. But, all the werewolves that were supposed to be there were gone. Not dispersed, we couldn't find them at all. We could track a lot of them congregating on the location where the meeting was supposed to be, but then pfft; like they suddenly disappeared, thus setting off the main story.<br /> <br /> All the way through, the ST really drew us in and made us feel like what we did mattered, even when we kind-of set off an Azlu plague. There were NPCs we were genuinely attached to, and enemies we found very satisfying to beat down. There was even some player-player conflict, such as the werebear convincing a peasant to throw rocks at the nobleman, and then getting upset and surprised when the nobleman killed the peasant, later stealing and breaking the nobles ancestral sword - that almost led to the party disintegrating, due to those two characters hating each other, and my character deciding they were both too unprofessional to work with. John couldn't really help with that, but he remained entirely impartial the whole time, which I can't imagine could have been easy.<br /> <br /> He later ran another game, which I also enjoyed. Unfortunately, I dropped out partway. Not because of the game, that was great, I'd just had enough of the behaviour of a couple of people in that house, player and non-player, and wanted some time away. I've since stopped gaming with or even talking to those people at all, and John later moved states for university, but he really was a great ST.<br /> <br /> So, your turn. Any great stories about STs?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-23T07:51:34-08:00","id":348324,"title":"","content":"Praises to my Pathfinder ST who has nearly killed me already twice. That might be a Stockholm Syndrome, but his GMing is almost perfect and even if we die at some point it will be still entertaining. For now, my Dwarf enjoys PTSD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-24T20:13:18-08:00","id":349486,"title":"","content":"Praises to my ST for VtM for being a champ and handling the wild ride of insanity that is my character. I didn't mean to take your special character and have her fall in love with me, but the love story is something that will strike fear into the hearts of Elders until the Modern nights.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>